module OpenerRunStaysOneRow exposing (..)

-- A run of ridable comments after a bracket's opener stays on ONE row when the
-- item below cannot join them.
--
-- Two paths render a bracketed literal's opener run, and they disagreed:
--
--   * the FLAT path (`glueLeadBoxes`), taken when no direct comment child forces
--     the bracket open -- it stacked each comment on a row of its own;
--   * the comment path (`glueLeadingCommentRun`), taken when one does -- it glues
--     the run onto one row above the body.
--
-- Which path runs is decided by the container constructor, so the author's
-- inline spelling took the first and its own output -- now `AlwaysVertical`,
-- because the items came to span rows -- took the second. The file never
-- settled.
--
-- What hid it is that the two trees are IDENTICAL apart from that constructor,
-- every comment role included. The multi-line `{- b -}` is a child of the ITEM,
-- not of the list, so the list's own comment children are two `RidesInline` ones
-- and `literalCommentsRideFlatLine` answers True.
-- `commentBracketListBox` had already named the hazard in so many words -- "one
-- riding while the other stacks is an oscillation with no role to blame".
--
-- Every comment here can share a line; only the BODY cannot join them. The
-- body's shape decides whether they may share ITS line, not whether they may
-- share each other's -- so they stay together. Found 2026-08-06 by
-- `fuzz-idempotency.py --gaps --mix`.


writtenInline =
    [ {- lambda -} {- a -} {- b
         second row -} \q -> q + one, other ]


-- The same content authored across rows, which is what the first one formats to.
-- Both must land on the same bytes; that they did not IS the bug.


writtenAcrossRows =
    [ {- lambda -}
      {- a -}
      {- b
         second row -}
      \q -> q + one
    , other
    ]


-- THE BOUNDARY: when the item can take the run on its own line, it still does --
-- the run rides the item rather than sitting above it.


itemTakesTheRun =
    [ {- lambda -} {- a -} plainItem, other ]
